 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Construction",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The control side is an LED; the load side is two MOSFETs in series between pins 3 and 4, switched by the LED's light across the isolation barrier. With no LED current the output is open; with the LED lit it conducts AC or DC load current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Switching times",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Turn-on time runs from the LED current step to the load current reaching 90 % of its final value; turn-off time runs from the LED current falling to the load current dropping to 10 %. Turn-on gets faster with more LED current and with lower temperature; turn-off gets slightly longer with more LED current and shorter as it gets hotter."
  },
  {
   "topic": "LED drive",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Up to 2 mA of LED current is needed to turn the relay on at 150 mA load at 25 °C, and below 0.2 mA it is guaranteed off. The current needed rises with temperature; above 60 °C at least 4 mA of drive is called for. The LED may be pulsed to 1 A for 10 ms, run at up to 50 mA continuously, and withstands 5 V reverse."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Load current derating",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Continuous load current is 150 mA up to 25 °C and falls linearly to 120 mA at 85 °C. Total dissipation is 400 mW at 25 °C, less 3.33 mW for each degree above. A 10 ms pulse of ±350 mA peak is allowed; the energy rating curve plots load current against time from 10 µs to 100 s."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Assembly",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Moisture sensitivity level 3. Peak body temperature in lead-free reflow is 260 °C, with at most 30 s within 5 °C of it and no more than three reflow passes. No-clean flux is preferred. If the board is washed, use a low-pressure wash and bake out trapped moisture afterwards; do not clean with ultrasound, and keep the part away from chlorine- or fluorine-based fluxes and solvents."
  }
